=== L2 Physical Development Across the Lifespan ===
id: 54268769-1160-403d-b9e1-c8c6d57fa463
slug: physical-development-across-the-lifespan
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:32:34.39271+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 31

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Reflexes, motor skills, puberty, menopause and ageing — physical change stage by stage.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Physical Development: A Story of Change</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Physical development is the most visible dimension of growth &mdash; the changes a person can see and feel in their own body. It follows a broadly predictable sequence from birth through to later life, though the pace and nature of change shift dramatically between the five life stages. Health and social care professionals &mdash; from health visitors monitoring infants to nurses on geriatric wards supporting older adults &mdash; use knowledge of physical development to assess how well an individual is doing for their age.</p>
<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Infancy (0&ndash;2 Years): Reflexes, Growth and First Skills</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Newborn babies arrive equipped with primitive <dfn class="term" data-def="Automatic, involuntary responses to stimuli that are present at birth. They are controlled by the brainstem and gradually disappear as the cortex develops.">reflexes</dfn> &mdash; automatic responses that help them survive. The <strong>rooting reflex</strong> turns a baby&rsquo;s head toward anything that strokes its cheek, supporting feeding. The <strong>grasp reflex</strong> causes the fingers to close around anything placed in the palm. The <strong>Moro (startle) reflex</strong> throws the arms outward in response to a sudden noise or drop. These reflexes fade over the first few months as voluntary control develops.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Physical growth is fastest in infancy. A typical baby doubles its birth weight in around five months and triples it by twelve months. Height increases by roughly 25 cm in the first year alone. The brain grows rapidly too &mdash; achieving around 90% of its adult volume by age two.</p>
<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and list three primitive reflexes present at birth, naming what triggers each one.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Newborns are born with primitive reflexes (rooting, grasp, Moro) that support survival. These fade as voluntary motor control develops &mdash; a sign of healthy brain maturation. Health visitors monitor this pattern at standard checks in the first year.</p>
</div>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Motor skills develop in a head-to-toe direction. <dfn class="term" data-def="Large-scale movements using the major muscle groups, such as sitting, crawling, standing, and walking.">Gross motor skills</dfn> include sitting unaided (around 6 months), crawling (around 9 months), pulling to standing (around 10 months), and walking independently (around 12&ndash;18 months). <dfn class="term" data-def="Precise, controlled movements using the smaller muscles of the hands and fingers, such as picking up objects and later holding a pencil.">Fine motor skills</dfn> develop alongside &mdash; an infant progresses from a palmar grasp (using the whole hand) to a pincer grasp (thumb and forefinger) by around 9&ndash;12 months.</p>
<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Childhood (3&ndash;10 Years): Growing Coordination and Self-Care</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Physical growth slows and becomes more steady during childhood. Children gain roughly 5&ndash;7 cm in height and 2&ndash;3 kg in weight per year. Coordination improves significantly &mdash; by age 5 a child can typically run, hop, and kick a ball with control.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Gross motor skills in childhood include skipping, riding a bike, throwing and catching accurately. Fine motor skills develop toward controlling a pencil, using scissors, and fastening buttons and laces. These <dfn class="term" data-def="Basic everyday tasks that an individual can perform without assistance, such as dressing, washing, and preparing simple food.">self-care skills</dfn> are important markers of independence that practitioners in early years settings observe carefully. Delays in fine motor development can indicate conditions such as dyspraxia and may prompt referral to an occupational therapist.</p>
<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Adolescence (11&ndash;19 Years): Puberty and the Growth Spurt</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">The most dramatic physical change in adolescence is <dfn class="term" data-def="The biological process by which a child's body develops into a sexually mature adult body, triggered by hormonal changes.">puberty</dfn>, driven by hormonal changes that cause rapid growth and the development of secondary sex characteristics. School nurses and GPs often provide advice and support during this transition.</p>
<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Puberty Changes in Girls and Boys</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13"><strong>In girls</strong>, puberty typically begins between ages 10&ndash;11. Changes include: breast development, widening of the hips, growth of pubic and underarm hair, the onset of menstruation (periods), and a growth spurt. Girls generally complete puberty by around age 14&ndash;16.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n14"><strong>In boys</strong>, puberty typically begins slightly later, around ages 11&ndash;13. Changes include: growth of the testes and penis, development of pubic, facial, and body hair, voice deepening (voice &ldquo;breaking&rdquo;), growth of muscle mass, and a later but more prolonged growth spurt. Boys generally complete puberty by around ages 15&ndash;17.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>
<p data-narration-id="n15">The adolescent growth spurt means a young person may gain 8&ndash;10 cm in height per year at its peak. Body composition changes significantly: boys gain muscle mass; girls gain body fat proportionally as the body prepares for potential pregnancy. Acne is common in both sexes as sebaceous glands become more active in response to hormonal changes.</p>
<h2 data-narration-id="n16">Adulthood (20&ndash;64 Years): Peak, Pregnancy and Menopause</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">Physical strength, cardiovascular fitness, and reaction speed all peak in the mid-twenties. This is the phase when the body performs at its optimum. From around age 30 onwards, gradual change begins: muscle mass decreases by roughly 3&ndash;5% per decade, skin elasticity reduces, and bone density begins to fall.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n18"><strong>Pregnancy</strong> is a significant physical experience during adulthood. The body undergoes major hormonal, cardiovascular, and physical changes to support the growing foetus. Blood volume increases by around 40&ndash;50%, the uterus expands from the size of a pear to accommodate a full-term baby, and weight typically increases by 10&ndash;12 kg. Midwives and obstetricians monitor these changes through antenatal care.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n19">The <dfn class="term" data-def="The natural end of a woman's menstrual cycles, typically occurring between ages 45 and 55, marking the end of reproductive capacity.">menopause</dfn> typically occurs between ages 45 and 55. The ovaries stop producing oestrogen, ending menstruation and fertility. Common symptoms include hot flushes, sleep disturbance, mood changes, and reduced bone density (increasing osteoporosis risk). GPs and women&rsquo;s health nurses often support women through this transition with advice on HRT (hormone replacement therapy) and lifestyle adjustments.</p>
<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n20" data-revision-tip="Close this and recall: in which life stage does the menopause typically occur? What are two physical effects it can cause?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The menopause occurs during adulthood (typically ages 45&ndash;55), not later adulthood. This is a common exam error. The drop in oestrogen increases the risk of osteoporosis and can cause symptoms including hot flushes and sleep disturbance.</p>
</div>
<h2 data-narration-id="n21">Later Adulthood (65+): The Ageing Process</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n22">The physical changes of later adulthood are largely the continuation and acceleration of changes that began in adulthood. However, in later adulthood they are more pronounced and more likely to affect independence and quality of life.</p>
<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Key Physical Effects of Ageing</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n23"><strong>Musculoskeletal changes:</strong> Muscle mass declines more rapidly (sarcopenia), joints stiffen due to cartilage wear, and bone density falls &mdash; raising the risk of osteoporosis and fractures, especially hip fractures in falls. Practitioners in residential care settings manage fall prevention programmes for this reason.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n24"><strong>Cardiovascular and respiratory changes:</strong> The heart muscle weakens slightly, blood vessels lose elasticity, and lung capacity reduces. This means older adults tire more quickly with exertion and may have reduced stamina.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n25"><strong>Sensory changes:</strong> Sight and hearing both commonly deteriorate. Presbyopia (difficulty focusing on close objects) and age-related hearing loss (presbycusis) are very common. Opticians and audiologists play important roles in supporting people at this life stage.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n26"><strong>Skin and appearance:</strong> Skin thins, loses moisture, and wrinkles. Hair whitens or thins. These changes are normal but can affect self-image and emotional wellbeing.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n28">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n29">Infancy features primitive reflexes, rapid weight gain, and the development of gross and fine motor skills, moving from palmar to pincer grasp.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n30">Puberty in adolescence drives rapid physical change &mdash; growth spurts and secondary sex characteristics. Peak physical capacity is reached in early adulthood (mid-twenties).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n31">The menopause (adulthood, 45&ndash;55) and the physical effects of ageing (later adulthood, 65+) &mdash; including bone density loss, reduced muscle mass, and sensory decline &mdash; are high-yield exam topics requiring precise stage-specific detail.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n27">A common mistake on physical development questions is to confuse which changes happen in <strong>adulthood</strong> versus <strong>later adulthood</strong>. The menopause, for example, is an adulthood event &mdash; not later adulthood. Similarly, peak physical strength is in early adulthood (mid-twenties), not adolescence. If a question uses an &lsquo;Explain&rsquo; command word, always include a &lsquo;because&rsquo; or &lsquo;this means that&rsquo; to link the physical change to its effect on the individual. A list of changes without reasoning will not reach 3 marks.</p>

