  "marks": "Up to 3 marks:\n• At the pre-conventional level, a child makes moral decisions based on self-interest (1) — for example, deciding not to steal because they fear punishment rather than because it harms others (1).\n• At the conventional level, moral decisions are based on social rules and the expectations of others (1), therefore the individual wants to follow laws and be seen as a good member of society rather than just avoiding personal consequences (1).\nMaximum 3 marks. Reason word required for the third mark."
 },
 {
  "text": "Describe, with an example, how a practitioner working in an early years setting could apply their knowledge of Piaget's concrete operational stage to support a child's learning.",
  "type": "4 marks — Describe with Example",
  "marks": "Up to 4 marks. 2 marks for the description; 2 marks for the applied example.\n• The concrete operational stage (ages 7–11) means children can think logically about real, observable events but struggle with abstract concepts (1) — therefore a practitioner should use hands-on, practical activities rather than purely verbal or abstract instruction (1).\n• For example, a teaching assistant could use physical counting objects to help a child understand maths rather than presenting sums in the abstract (1), because the child can reason logically about what they can see and manipulate directly (1)."
 },
 {
  "text": "Theo is 9 years old and lives in a foster placement. His foster carer has noticed he finds it difficult to see other people's points of view during arguments with other children and becomes frustrated when rules change without explanation. His school has asked a learning support assistant to work with him.\n\nUsing Piaget's stages of cognitive development, analyse how Theo's cognitive development might be influencing his behaviour, and explain what this means for how the learning support assistant should support him.",
  "type": "8 marks — Analyse",
  "marks": "Mastering (7–8): Accurate identification of Theo's likely Piaget stage (concrete operational, possibly transitioning from pre-operational given some egocentric tendencies). Detailed analysis linking stage features to described behaviours. Clear, specific advice for the learning support assistant grounded in Piaget's framework. Consistent reference to Theo's circumstances (foster placement, arguments, rule changes).\n\nSecure (5–6): Accurate stage identification with reasonable analysis and some practical advice for the practitioner. Limited development of the emotional/social implications.\n\nDeveloping (3–4): Some accurate Piaget content but analysis descriptive rather than applied to Theo. Advice is generic.\n\nEmerging (1–2): Basic Piaget recall with little application.\n\nIndicative content:\n• At 9, Theo is in the concrete operational stage (7–11) — he can think logically about concrete events but may still show some egocentric tendencies from the pre-operational stage.\n• Difficulty seeing others' viewpoints links to residual egocentric thinking — he interprets situations from his own perspective, which can appear as selfishness but is a cognitive characteristic of this developmental stage.\n• Frustration at rule changes links to the concrete operational child's need for clear, consistent, observable rules — abstract or frequently changing rules are cognitively difficult to process.\n• The learning support assistant should use clear, step-by-step explanations of rules and consequences (concrete, not abstract), role-play activities to develop perspective-taking, and consistent routines that give Theo cognitive predictability.\n• The foster placement context is relevant — children who have experienced disruption may show developmental delays; the practitioner should not assume Theo is operating at the top of the concrete operational stage."
 }
