  "type": "6 marks — Discuss",
  "marks": "Mastering (5–6): Correctly identifies Jaylen's behaviour as consistent with secure attachment. Discusses both what this predicts for positive future development AND acknowledges that attachment is not destiny (other life experiences also matter). Consistently references Jaylen's specific circumstances and care setting context.\n\nSecure (3–4): Correct identification of secure attachment with some discussion of implications for future development. May not fully address counter-considerations.\n\nDeveloping (2): Identifies the correct attachment type with limited discussion.\n\nEmerging (1): Names an attachment type with no discussion or limited accuracy.\n\nIndicative content:\n• Jaylen's behaviour — distress at separation but easy comfort on reunion and return to play — is the defining profile of secure attachment (Ainsworth's Strange Situation).\n• Secure attachment suggests Jaylen has a consistent, responsive primary caregiver; his internal working model (Bowlby) will be one of trust and reliability.\n• Predicted positive outcomes: tends toward higher self-esteem, more trusting peer relationships in childhood, better capacity for intimate partnerships in adulthood.\n• The role of the nursery key-worker as a secondary attachment figure is also relevant — a secure base in the nursery setting supports separation.\n• Counter-consideration: attachment type is not fixed — adverse life experiences (bereavement, family breakdown) can alter the trajectory; positive relationships later can also partially compensate for early insecure attachment."
 },
