  "type": "8 marks — Analyse",
  "marks": "Mastering (7–8): Detailed analysis drawing on both Bowlby and Bandura with accurate theoretical content. Chains early attachment disruption to Nadia's current difficulties with trust and peer relationships. Addresses the social learning dimension (pupil referral unit peer environment, role models). Consistently applied to Nadia's circumstances throughout.\n\nSecure (5–6): Accurate use of both theories with clear links to Nadia. Some chaining. May not fully integrate both theories.\n\nDeveloping (3–4): Accurate theoretical content but applied descriptively rather than analytically. May address only one theory.\n\nEmerging (1–2): Basic theoretical recall with minimal application to Nadia.\n\nIndicative content:\nBowlby application:\n• Foster care from ages 1–3 falls within Bowlby's critical period (0–3 years) for attachment formation. Disruption to the primary attachment during this period is associated with lasting emotional and social consequences.\n• Nadia's difficulty trusting adults is consistent with Bowlby's concept of the internal working model — her early experience of inconsistent or disrupted caregiving may have produced a working model of adults as unreliable or threatening.\n• This affects her emotional wellbeing (inability to form secure bonds) and social development (cannot build stable friendships, which require trust).\nBandura application:\n• The pupil referral unit peer environment is significant. If the dominant peer models demonstrate anti-social behaviour, poor academic engagement, or conflict with authority figures, Nadia is likely to observe and imitate these behaviours through vicarious reinforcement — especially if those behaviours are rewarded with peer status or approval.\n• Positive role models from practitioners in the unit (key workers, mentors) can provide alternative modelling — if Nadia observes consistent, trustworthy adult behaviour, social learning theory suggests this can begin to counteract her negative internal working model.\nIntegration:\n• Bowlby explains WHY Nadia struggles with trust (disrupted early attachment); Bandura explains HOW her current environment shapes what behaviours she is learning now. Both frameworks point to the importance of stable, consistent, trusted adult relationships in the pupil referral unit as the primary mechanism for supporting her recovery."
