=== L5 Life Factors — What Shapes Health Across the Lifespan ===
id: a3d41ca3-a9a3-47c0-982d-b499647f2da9
slug: life-factors-what-shapes-health-across-the-lifespan
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:44:21.510902+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 30

--- DESCRIPTION ---
All thirteen spec life factors — abuse, access, economic, environment, family, identity and more.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is a Life Factor?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A condition or circumstance that an individual is born into or finds themselves in, which influences their growth, development and health outcomes across the lifespan.">life factor</dfn> is a condition or circumstance &mdash; not a choice, but a given &mdash; that shapes how a person grows, develops, and experiences health. The spec identifies thirteen named life factors, grouped broadly into areas covering relationships, access to services, economic conditions, the living environment, personal health, and identity. Health and social care practitioners need to understand these factors because many of the individuals they support have been significantly affected by them.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Life factors differ from lifestyle choices (such as diet or exercise, covered in the next lesson). A child does not choose their family structure, their neighbourhood, or whether they experience abuse &mdash; these are factors imposed on them. Recognising this distinction matters in practice: a key-worker in a residential care home who understands the life factors a resident has faced can provide more empathetic, person-centred support.</p>
<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Abuse</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Abuse is one of the most damaging life factors an individual can experience. The spec identifies seven distinct types: <strong>physical abuse</strong> (causing bodily harm), <strong>emotional abuse</strong> (persistent criticism, humiliation, rejection or threats), <strong>sexual abuse</strong> (any sexual act without consent), <strong>verbal abuse</strong> (using words to belittle, demean or control), <strong>neglect</strong> (failure to meet basic physical or emotional needs), <strong>coercive control</strong> (a pattern of behaviour that controls, threatens or frightens a person), and <strong>financial abuse</strong> (misusing or stealing someone&rsquo;s money or assets).</p>
<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write from memory all seven types of abuse listed in the spec. Check your list against the lesson.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The seven types of abuse are: physical, emotional, sexual, verbal, neglect, coercive control, and financial. Any form of abuse has profound effects across all PIES domains &mdash; physical injury, intellectual disruption, emotional trauma, and social withdrawal are all documented consequences. Practitioners have a <strong>duty to report</strong> concerns to safeguarding leads.</p>
</div>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Each type of abuse affects PIES development in interconnected ways. Neglect during infancy, for example, disrupts attachment formation (emotional), impairs brain development (intellectual), delays physical growth, and limits socialisation (social). Coercive control in an adult relationship can affect physical health (through enforced isolation or withheld medication), intellectual development (restricted access to education or work), emotional wellbeing (persistent fear and low self-esteem), and social contacts (enforced isolation from friends and family).</p>
<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Access to Services</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9"><dfn class="term" data-def="The extent to which an individual can make use of health, social care, and community services that support their growth, development, and well-being.">Access to services</dfn> is a significant life factor because unequal access produces unequal outcomes. The spec highlights immunisations and screening programmes, early years services (children&rsquo;s centres, nurseries), youth services, and day centres. Children who miss immunisations face higher disease risk; adults who cannot access mental health services may deteriorate without support; older adults who have no access to a day centre may face severe social isolation.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Barriers to access include geography (rural areas with fewer services), financial cost (transport, prescription charges), language barriers, disability, and lack of knowledge about what services exist. The Marmot Review (2010) highlighted that inequalities in access to services are a major driver of health inequalities across the lifespan in the UK.</p>
<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Economic Factors</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Economic factors &mdash; including employment, income, wages, benefits, savings, and material possessions &mdash; are among the most powerful determinants of health. The spec distinguishes <strong>absolute poverty</strong> (lacking the basic necessities for survival) from <strong>relative poverty</strong> (having significantly less than the average standard of living in your society).</p>
<p data-narration-id="n13">Poverty affects PIES across the lifespan. Children growing up in low-income households are more likely to have poor nutrition, live in damp or overcrowded housing, attend underfunded schools, and experience the emotional stress of financial insecurity &mdash; all of which affect their cognitive development and social participation. Adults in unemployment face not only financial hardship but loss of routine, social contacts, and sense of purpose, which can significantly damage emotional wellbeing. The standard of living and quality of housing are directly linked to physical health outcomes &mdash; cold, damp homes increase respiratory illness; overcrowding increases infection spread.</p>
<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Educational Experiences</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">Educational experiences &mdash; including the quality of schooling, experiences of bullying, and the influence of peers &mdash; shape intellectual, emotional, and social development significantly. A child who is bullied at school may avoid attendance, miss learning, experience anxiety and low self-esteem, and withdraw from peer groups &mdash; affecting all four PIES domains. A child who receives strong, inclusive educational support is more likely to develop confidence, communication skills, and academic achievement that open future employment opportunities.</p>
<h2 data-narration-id="n16">Living Environment</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">The physical environment in which a person lives is a life factor they typically do not control. Relevant environmental factors include: <strong>access to green space</strong> (linked to mental well-being and physical activity), <strong>overcrowding</strong> (affects sleep, stress levels, and infection risk), <strong>unsafe conditions</strong> (structural hazards in housing, dangerous neighbourhoods), and <strong>pollution</strong> (both air pollution, which damages respiratory health, and noise pollution, which disrupts sleep and concentration).</p>
<h2 data-narration-id="n18">Family and Relationships</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n19">Family structure and parenting style are significant life factors. The spec names several family types: nuclear (two parents and children), extended (including grandparents or other relatives), single-parent, step, foster, adoptive, and same-sex parent families. No family type is inherently better for development &mdash; the quality of relationships within the family matters more than its structure.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n20">Parenting style is a particularly well-researched influence on child development. The spec identifies four main styles:</p>
<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Four Parenting Styles and Their Effects</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n21"><strong>Authoritative</strong>: high warmth, clear rules with explanation. Associated with positive outcomes: children tend to develop high self-esteem, good social skills, and academic confidence. <strong>Authoritarian</strong>: strict rules, low warmth, obedience demanded without explanation. Can produce well-behaved children but also lower self-esteem and limited autonomy. <strong>Neglectful</strong>: low warmth, low control; the parent is largely disengaged. Associated with poor outcomes across all PIES domains. <strong>Indulgent</strong>: high warmth, low rules; the child gets what they want without learning boundaries. Can lead to difficulties with self-regulation and following rules in school and later life.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>
<h2 data-narration-id="n22">Gender, Sexuality, and Long-Term Conditions</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n23">Gender and <dfn class="term" data-def="A person's enduring pattern of emotional, romantic, and/or sexual attraction to others, which may be a life factor influencing their experiences of discrimination or social support.">sexuality</dfn> can be life factors where they expose individuals to discrimination, prejudice, or exclusion. LGBT+ individuals may face stigma in schools, workplaces, and some community or family settings, which can significantly affect emotional wellbeing, social development, and mental health.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n24">Long-term and life-limiting conditions are also named life factors. These include <strong>congenital conditions</strong> (present from birth), <strong>genetic conditions</strong>, <strong>chronic illness</strong> (long-term conditions such as Type 1 diabetes or epilepsy), and <strong>comorbidity</strong> (having more than one long-term condition at the same time). <dfn class="term" data-def="The co-occurrence of two or more long-term health conditions in the same individual, which can complicate diagnosis, treatment, and care planning.">Comorbidity</dfn> is increasingly common in older adults and significantly increases the complexity of care needs.</p>
<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Culture, Race, Learning Disabilities and Sensory Impairments as Life Factors</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n25">Culture and race are life factors where they shape an individual&rsquo;s experiences of discrimination and prejudice. Ethnic minority individuals in the UK are statistically more likely to experience socioeconomic disadvantage, housing inequality, and healthcare disparities &mdash; all of which create additional barriers to health. Learning disabilities (including conditions on the autism spectrum) and behavioural disorders (such as ADHD) affect intellectual and social development. Sensory impairments &mdash; hearing and sight loss &mdash; limit access to information, education, and social participation without appropriate support from practitioners and services.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>
<div class="key-fact" data-revision-tip="In a marked question, never list factors in isolation &mdash; always link the factor to a developmental milestone it could disrupt."><div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div><p>The spec rewards <strong>linkage</strong>: economic disadvantage doesn&rsquo;t just mean &lsquo;less money&rsquo; &mdash; it links to nutrition, housing quality, exposure to stress, and access to enrichment activities, all of which delay or disrupt PIES milestones in specific named ways.</p></div>


--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n27">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n28">The spec names thirteen distinct life factors: abuse (seven types), access to services, economic factors, educational experiences, living environment, family and relationships (including four parenting styles), culture and race, gender and sexuality, long-term conditions, learning disabilities, behavioural disorders, mental health, and sensory impairments.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n29">Life factors are conditions individuals are born into or find themselves in &mdash; not choices. They affect all four PIES domains and often interact: poverty limits access to services, which worsens health outcomes, which increases economic burden.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n30">Recognising life factors helps practitioners provide person-centred care &mdash; understanding why an individual&rsquo;s development has followed a particular path, rather than making assumptions about individual behaviour or motivation.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n26">Questions on life factors commonly use a case-study format and ask you to &lsquo;identify&rsquo; or &lsquo;explain&rsquo; a life factor affecting the named individual. The most common mistake is confusing a life factor with a lifestyle choice &mdash; remember: a life factor is something the individual does not choose (their family, their neighbourhood, a disability), while a lifestyle choice is a behaviour they elect (their diet, whether they smoke). For 3-mark &lsquo;Explain&rsquo; questions, name the life factor, state its effect, and add a &lsquo;because&rsquo; or &lsquo;this means that&rsquo; to develop it into a third mark. Do not list multiple factors without developing any &mdash; depth beats breadth for this question type.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
