  "text": "Describe, with examples from a named care setting, how formal and informal care work together to support a child with a learning disability who has not reached expected intellectual and social milestones.",
  "type": "4 marks — Describe with Example",
  "marks": "Up to 4 marks. 2 marks for formal care with setting-linked example; 2 marks for informal care with complementary example.\n• Formal care: in a specialist primary school or children's centre, an educational psychologist and teaching assistant work together to assess the child's needs and provide adapted learning activities that build on their current level (1). A speech and language therapist may provide targeted sessions to support communication development (1).\n• Informal care: at home, the child's parents use techniques modelled by the therapist — for example, practising Makaton signing during daily routines (1) — providing consistent reinforcement that extends the benefit of formal therapy into the child's home environment (1)."
 },
 {
  "text": "Marcus Griffiths is 68 years old. He was recently discharged from hospital following a stroke that has left him with weakness on his left side and significant communication difficulties. He lives with his daughter Seren, who works part-time. Marcus finds it hard to express his care preferences to professionals. The local authority has arranged a care package.\n\nUsing your knowledge of formal and informal support, evaluate how different types of support could help Marcus meet his developmental needs following the stroke.",
  "type": "10 marks — Evaluate (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering (9–10): Comprehensive evaluation covering formal care (named professionals with specific functions), informal care (Seren's role, its limitations), advocacy, equipment, financial support, and at least one other support type. Reaches a reasoned overall judgement about the balance and limitations of support. Consistently applied to Marcus's circumstances throughout.\n\nSecure (7–8): Good coverage of formal and informal care with some named professionals and applied examples. Addresses at least three support types. Some evaluative comment but may not balance positive and limitations or reach a clear judgement.\n\nDeveloping (4–6): Accurate content but may list types without evaluating. May not address the limitation of Seren as informal carer or acknowledge potential gaps in provision.\n\nEmerging (1–3): Basic points. Limited application to Marcus.\n\nIndicative content:\nFormal care:\n• Physiotherapist: supports rehabilitation of left-side weakness through targeted exercises — directly addresses physical developmental need (P).\n• Speech and language therapist: supports Marcus's communication difficulties, enabling him to express needs and preferences — critical given his communication barriers (I and E).\n• GP and district nurse: ongoing monitoring of health and medication; coordinating further referrals if needed.\n• Occupational therapist: assesses and adapts Marcus's home environment (e.g. grab rails, adapted kitchen equipment) to support independence in later adulthood.\n\nInformal care:\n• Seren provides daily personal care and emotional support — essential but demanding. Her part-time work limits availability; without respite care, she risks carer burnout which would reduce the quality of support Marcus receives.\n• Respite care or a carers' assessment would identify Seren's own needs and provide formal breaks.\n\nAdvocacy:\n• Marcus's communication difficulty means he struggles to express care preferences. A professional advocate ensures his wishes are represented in care planning meetings — supporting his right to person-centred care and his emotional wellbeing and sense of control.\n\nEquipment and financial support:\n• Mobility aids (walking frame, wheelchair) and home adaptations (wet room, raised toilet) support physical independence. These may be funded through Attendance Allowance or a local authority community care grant.\n• Personal Independence Payment may supplement income if Marcus can no longer work.\n\nSupport groups:\n• A stroke survivors' group provides social contact, information from others who have recovered, and emotional validation — reducing isolation.\n\nEvaluation / judgement:\n• Formal and informal care together are more effective than either alone — therapy is only beneficial if supported by consistent practice at home. However, the burden on Seren is significant and sustainable only with respite and financial support. The advocacy role is particularly important for Marcus given his communication difficulty — without it, his formal care risks being planned without his input, reducing its effectiveness and his sense of dignity and control."
